if you haven't yet seen my video on the  index of hydrogen deficiency for  hydrocarbons take a moment to watch that  now determining unsaturation from a  chemical formula is simple enough when  we have a hydrocarbon but what happens  when other elements are present we know  from our bonding lesson that the most  likely elements in organic molecules are  concentrated in that upper right of the  periodic table carbon likes to make four  bonds when neutral nitrogen three oxygen  two and the halogens one for this part  of the lesson let's abbreviate the  halogens as X a very common abbreviation  okay if any of the elements I've shown  here are present we're going to need to  correct the saturated formula that we  originally calculate now for nitrogen  we're going to add one hydrogen atom to  the formula moving over one if we have  an oxygen atom do nothing to the  original saturated formula we won't add  or subtract anything at all now if there  are any halogens present in the formula  for each halogen subtract one hydrogen  atom  now this correction has to do with the  fact that nitrogen makes three bonds  oxygen two and the halogens one when  they are neutral let's see why by  looking at a few examples let's keep it  simple with examples containing two  carbon atoms ethane our saturated  hydrocarbon example is c2h6  and it looks like this okay so say we  add a nitrogen atom here we're going to  have to add one hydrogen to our  saturated formula giving us h7 okay  let's look at some possible structures  for this compound to figure out why we  had to add that H atom if we imagine  adding the nitrogen in the middle of  ethane we'll get this structure here's  our skeleton and nitrogen likes to have  three bonds when neutral so there's one  H atom coming off of it  if we count up our hydrogen atoms we  have three six seven there's one more  possibility for our structure if we put  the nitrogen at the end here's our  skeleton I filled in the hydrogen atoms  on carbon and then of course our  nitrogen likes to have three bonds it  needs to have two hydrogen's on it so we  have three four five six seven hydrogen  atoms in this structure as well all  right when we add an oxygen atom in we  don't have to change our formula so  let's look at c2h6o for our first  structure let's imagine popping the  oxygen in between the two carbons since  oxygen likes to make two bonds when  neutral when we pop it in there it has a  bond to each carbon atom and therefore  doesn't change the formula we don't need  to add or subtract any hydrogen's again  with this formula there's one more  possibility for the structure where we  put the hetero atom the oxygen atom on  the end our terminal carbon atom has  three hydrogen's to give it four bonds  this carbon needs two and we need to add  one hydrogen atom to oxygen since it  likes to have two bonds when neutral so  this structure which is dimethyl ether  or this structure which is ethanol are  both possibilities for c2h6o they  imagine we add a halogen now let's add  bromine when we do this we'll need to  subtract one H atom from our formula so  our formula is C 2 H 5b are the halogens  like to make one bond when they are  neutral so the only place we can put the  BR is on the end of the compound we  can't sandwich it in between two carbons  or anything like that so we'll just have  one structure we have one bond to this  carbon and two bonds to this so let's  fill in so that we have four bonds so  basically in this structure since  bromine makes one bond it's replacing a  hydrogen atom so we subtract one  hydrogen atom from our saturated formula  when we add bromine let's do a couple i  HD problems to be sure we have this down  for example 1 let's do C 2 H 4 o  remember our equation to figure out  saturated compounds is  - n + 2 filling in for 2 carbon and then  to correct for oxygen we do nothing so  this is going to be the number that we  use now let's do our ihd calculation so  we take our 6 expected hydrogen atoms  for the saturated formula subtract it by  the hydrogen's in our actual formula and  divide by 2  and this gives us a degree of  unsaturation of 1  so we can have one double bond or one  ring there are a few structural  possibilities for this formula let's  start by looking at double bonds first  let's imagine a double bond between the  two carbons we need to add two groups  onto this carbon this carbon already has  three bonds so we'll add one on and  oxygen likes to make two bonds when  neutral so we just need to add one bond  onto the oxygen filling in our hydrogen  atoms we can see we have four ok oxygen  makes two bonds when neutral so it's  possible the double bond is to the  oxygen atom that gives us this skeleton  we don't need to add anything onto  oxygen because it already has its two  bonds this terminal carbon needs three  groups and this central carbon has three  bonds and only needs one again let's add  in our hydrogen atoms so for our double  bond examples we have two possibilities  this compound which is called an e all  in this compound which is called an  aldehyde now it's also possible that we  have a ring we can pop an oxygen atom  into the ring because it bonds in two  directions we can see oxygen is  satisfied with its two bonds but we need  to add two bonds onto each carbon this  Epoque side also satisfies our  structural formula now let's do an  example where we need to correct our  saturated formula okay we have three  carbons let's plug that into our  equation here's our saturated equation  this will give us eight hydrogen atoms  but now we need to correct for bromine  so we need to subtract one hydrogen atom  from our calculation that we just did  this is because bromine is going to  replace a hydrogen atom so with our  correction for our saturated formula  we're expecting seven H atoms now let's  do our i HD calculation using this  corrected number so we have seven minus  five which gives us to divide that by  two and we have an I HD of one we have  three carbon atoms let's start by  drawing a structure that has a ring this  time each carbon is bonded two ways and  therefore needs two more substituents we  can put the bromine  on any one of the carbon atoms and then  fill in the rest of our hydrogen's we  can also have a double bond between  carbon atoms let's fill in four bonds  for each carbon atom now we have a lot  of possibilities for this compound the  bromine can be placed on any of these  carbon atoms and we get a distinct  compound if we place it on this terminus  the central carbon or this terminus  let's put it in the center as one of our  examples so with the double bond we have  our five expected hydrogen atoms and to  complete our story here are a couple  other structural possibilities by  putting the bromine in different spots  in this lesson we looked at how to  calculate the number of hydrogen atoms  on a saturated hydrocarbon using the  number of carbon atoms in the structure  we looked at how to correct our formula  for some of the common elements we'll  see in organic compounds we looked at  the structural origin of this and did a  few practice problems KP here if you  learn something give me a thumbs up on  the way out and for more chemistry  subscribe to my channel
 

